+## Implemented native presentation and ownership
+
+- Twelve registered definitions preserve the three stock challenges and current native 1-Up; eight adapters add Spot Jam, Hall of Meat, Deathrace, Domination, Graffiti, Skate Tag, Infection, and Hide & Seek. Imported legacy 1-Up/S.K.A.T.E. are not alternate implementations.
+- New native card models are private clones, appended idempotently. Their title, description, artwork, callback, and settings are separate from the stock models. Selection and integer settings use native Rime tile/list/selector widgets.
+- New challenge scores, clocks, status, standings, intro/countdown and completed-match leaderboard use the game's existing widgets and authored style/model records. World objectives use projected native notice widgets driven by route/capture/tag/player coordinates. Placement instructions use the actual controls supplied by the mode runtime.
+- Hide & Seek's blindness uses a private copy of the native teleport-fade fill; its focus and navigation links are removed. Input remains locked during hiding time, and hider names/map indicators/chat bubbles are suppressed.
+- The supplied ImGui Game Modes HUD/menu and Bone Cam HUD renderers are not compiled or activated. Shared snapshot/input helper types from `Overlay` are data/input contracts, not HUD renderers. Existing ReSkate chat, console, and unrelated tools are retained.
+- Countdown, teleport, session marker, cancellation and map-change ownership are coordinated with 1-Up and stock native challenges. End/error paths release mode locks, placements and forced Hall of Meat state. Mode messages now use version 10 of the private `0xD5` protocol for per-racer clocks and explicit finish-line state. Upstream multiplayer protocol is 47 after the v2.0.3 update; the existing 1-Up wire format remains separate. No new public module-loading ABI is invented.
+
+## Artwork and asset pipeline
+
+One generated transparent 4-by-2 sheet is saved as `assets/throwdowns/placeholder-badges.png`. Top row: Spot Jam (pink), Hall of Meat (orange), Deathrace (red), Domination (violet). Bottom row: Graffiti (turquoise), Skate Tag (yellow), Infection (green), Hide & Seek (cyan). No further image iterations were generated.
+
+`../ReSkateStudio/src/cli/one_up_badge.cpp` accepts this optional atlas after the existing 1-Up PNG and project arguments. It slices proportional cell bounds, encodes private native BC3-sRGB textures with transparency, validates all ten original Throwdown defaults are unchanged, and adds native HUD residency plus private elimination-line and blackout widgets. The source project/mod and encoded previews are under `../../outputs/Throwdown-artwork/`.
+
+Compile the native project with `reskate_cli compile-mod`, then run `../build_oneup_native_bundle.py` on its `compiled/Patch`. CMake embeds the verified archive in the runtime's `ONE_UP_NATIVE_ASSETS` resource. The core loader extracts it to `Mods/.reskate-core/one-up-native-v1/Patch` and mounts that directory automatically; players do not enable a separate third-party logo/HUD mod.
+
+## Explicit limits and follow-up validation
+
+The imported scoring adapters retain their supplied Trainer telemetry for Spot Jam/Graffiti; this is not a claim that those modes already use the exact native 1-Up landed-trick counter. Deathrace now uses native tutorial checkpoint meshes and native Spot Battle start-flag placement; capture/territory visuals still use projected native notices. Infection's rules and native infected markers are wired, but the supplied remote Grim Reaper costume helper is absent from this source snapshot and was not fabricated. Optional bail-bounce hooks report their absence. Existing 1-Up's unfinished pause Throwdown Panel route and native winner-emote/showcase camera sequence still need verified engine integration. The native results leaderboard does not imply that those cinematics have been implemented.
+
+Live validation should exercise each added card, native focus/controls, required route or spot placement, start/countdown, data updates, natural completion, quitting and another challenge afterward. Use peers running the same build for the new mode protocol; validate late joining, leader loss, session/map changes, and no surviving HUD/input state. Any observed defect must be fixed and rechecked before declaring the full system polished or multiplayer-compatible.
